  "text": "Samhain is a Celtic holiday. It falls on November 1 and opens the winter and the start of the year. The Celts counted the new year from Samhain, much as each day began at dusk. On Samhain the boundary between the living and the dead grew thin. The dead, fairies, and witches all walked abroad. People lit fires and told the future. Divination was a common rite. In Ireland a heifer increased in value on Samain, in the reckoning of ancient law. The Gaulish month-name Samoni, from the Coligny calendar, matches the Irish Samain. A three-night Samhain is written there. Samhain survived to become the modern Halloween. It also reached the Day of the Dead among later peoples in Spain and the Americas.",
